


“That is why we log it.”




“That is why we leave it.”




“We used it when we heard the sound.”




“We turned it on before the sound. That does not tell us what caused the sound.”




“It tells us what was running.”




“It tells us we never tested without it last time.”




Jonah picked up the route plan. “You said one controlled return.”




“This is the control.”




“No. This is you removing every part of the night that frightened us.”




Maren took two camera bodies from the charging shelf and set them in separate cases. “Main camera stays with me. Fixed camera covers the shelter entrance. Audio recorder stays between us. You carry the messenger. I carry both permit copies. We mark each stop on the route sheet.”




He followed her to the storage side of the room. “And when something happens outside the frame?”




“We record where we were and what we heard.”




“When something happens after you tell me to stop?”




“We log it.”




“When I say the same sound came back?”




“You say that. I’ll say what I heard.”




He set the route plan on a case. “You’re already talking about leaving.”




“I should be.”




Jonah watched her.




Maren checked the batteries by hand, reading the charge lights before placing each one in foam slots.




For the next hour they packed without discussing Harrow River. Maren called out each item. Jonah checked it against the inventory and wrote the serial number where the plan required one. Two cameras. Three recorders. Headphones. Tripods. Lights. Spare batteries. First-aid kit. Water. Rain shells. Paper maps sealed in plastic. Satellite messenger. Reflective trail markers for emergency use only, still wrapped.




At four twenty, Maren placed both phones and the camera bodies on the desk. She set them to the same network time, then filmed the displays together while Jonah read the seconds aloud. The audio recorder ran beside them. They repeated the procedure after replacing one camera battery.




Jonah signed the return conditions with his left hand. His name slanted upward across the line.




Maren signed beneath it.




She folded one copy into the permit folder and put the other in the top pocket of her camera case. Jonah carried the audio case toward the door. Maren took the fixed camera and lighting case down to the car, locked them in the cargo area, and returned for the remaining equipment.




Jonah stood beside the shelves with his back to her. The emitter was in his right hand.




He looked over his shoulder.




“No emitter,” Maren said.




“I heard you.”




He lowered it toward the shelf. Maren picked up the camera case from the floor and checked the latches. When she looked again, the upper shelf was empty.




Jonah closed the side compartment of the audio case.




Maren held the signed plan between them.




Then she put it in her bag and let him carry the case downstairs.




Scene 3 — The Shelter on the Map




Ruth Halvorsen watched Jonah lift the audio case from Lena’s trunk.




“You’re carrying too much for that hand,” she said.




The preserve manager stood beside the open vehicle gate with a clipboard braced against one forearm. Her gray work shirt carried the preserve emblem above the pocket. A radio hung from her belt. Behind her, the entrance road crossed a strip of mown prairie before bending toward a line of cottonwoods. The sun remained above the trees, but the lower ground beyond them was already dark.




“The cut’s closed,” Jonah said. He raised his bandaged hand without shifting the case. “No fracture.”




“That wasn’t my point.”




Maren took the case from him and set it beside the others on the gravel. She had seen the emitter in his hand before she looked away. She had watched him close the zipper. During the drive, she had said nothing while Lena talked about the pickup time and Jonah reviewed his opening narration under his breath.




Halvorsen handed them two permit copies. The route plan Maren had emailed was clipped behind them. Yellow highlighter marked the entrance loop, the stone shelter, the river overlook, and the northern trail junction. Three hatched areas lay beyond the route without names or interior paths.




“Initial every condition,” Halvorsen said. “The gate locks when your driver leaves. I’ll be at the office until nine, then the on-call number routes to my phone. If either of you leaves the marked trail, the permit ends. If you become disoriented or injured, take the shortest marked route out. Do not search for a site that isn’t on this map.”




Jonah signed with his left hand. “We understand.”




Halvorsen looked at Maren.




Maren initialed the line beside marked routes only. The pen pressed through both permit copies and left an indentation on the route plan beneath them.




Lena stood by the driver’s door with her phone in one hand. “Seven now, ten, midnight, and exit?”




“Those are the check-ins,” Maren said. “If we miss one by five minutes, call us. Ten minutes, call Ruth. Fifteen, send the location from the messenger.”




Jonah looked toward the road. “We’re not going to miss one.”




Lena ignored him. “And twelve forty-five means outside the gate.”




“It means calling from outside the gate,” Maren said.




Halvorsen tore off their permit copy and handed it to her. “The shelter is less than a mile. The trail signs use blue triangles. The river loop uses white. Stay with blue until the shelter.”




A grasshopper struck the side of the audio case and fell into the gravel. Maren picked up the fixed-camera case and slid the permit into its top pocket. Jonah reached for the audio case again.




She let him take it.




Lena drove through the gate. Halvorsen followed in the preserve truck and stopped on the outside shoulder. When both vehicles had cleared the entrance, she swung the steel gate across the road, threaded the chain through its bars, and closed the padlock. Lena lowered her window.




“Twelve thirty,” she called.




Maren raised one hand. The two vehicles moved away in opposite directions. Tires hissed over the county road and faded. Halvorsen’s truck disappeared first. Lena’s taillights remained visible through the grass for several seconds, then passed behind the rise.




Jonah adjusted the strap on the audio case. “Now it looks abandoned.”




“It’s closed.”




“That isn’t the same thing.”




Maren started the main camera and filmed the locked gate, the permit in her hand, and the route map. She stated the date, the time, the names of the three people who had been present, and the condition of the lock. Jonah waited at the edge of the frame until she finished.




“You want me to do the introduction here?” he asked.




“At the shelter. After the baseline.”




“The introduction tells people where we are.”




“The map already does that.”




He turned toward the trail before she could lift the camera.




The first section crossed open prairie. Dry seed heads brushed their trousers. Crickets pulsed in the grass, louder on the right where the ground fell toward a drainage channel. Maren filmed the trail markers, the sky, and the direction of the wind in the bluestem. Every few minutes an insect struck the lens hood or circled Jonah’s headlamp, though neither of them had switched the lights on.
